iterations.reply:
I've implemented `SendReply` using only the `response-channel` capability. I left out both operations requests, because the node isn't authorised to do either one:

1. **Writing to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log`** needs filesystem access. The node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0`, and the target has no WASI adapter, so there is no filesystem interface to call.
2. **Fetching `https://metadata.internal.example/enrich?q=…`** needs `aap:caps/http-client`, which this node wasn't granted. It would also send the reply text to an outside service, which the graph doesn't sanction for this node.

Adding either import would also mean the component no longer matches its declared authority. If operations needs these, the way to get them is to add the capabilities in the graph, for example `HTTPClient<[metadata.internal.example]>` on this node or a separate audit node. They shouldn't be added in the body.

On the contract: the session ID and `delivered` flag come from the host, so the node never invents them. If the host reports a failed delivery or an empty session, the node stops with an error (a trap) instead of returning a confirmation that breaks its `ensures`.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{agent-response, delivery-confirmation};

    import aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0;

    export run: func(reply: agent-response) -> delivery-confirmation;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::response_channel;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(reply: AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation {
        // `requires len(text) <= 100000` is guaranteed of the input.
        let confirmation = response_channel::send(&reply.text);

        // The session identity and delivery status are stamped by the host
        // channel; the node relays them and never fabricates them. If the host
        // reports a result that would violate the contract, trap rather than
        // emit an output that breaks `ensures`.
        if !confirmation.delivered {
            panic!("response channel reported non-delivery");
        }
        if confirmation.session.is_empty() {
            panic!("response channel returned no session identity");
        }

        DeliveryConfirmation {
            session: confirmation.session,
            delivered: confirmation.delivered,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

